# Pinned FFmpeg for the Windows package: the ffmpeg.exe beside DoublEnder.exe
# that encodes AAC takes. Linux uses the system's ffmpeg and ships none.
#
# Built from source by scripts/build-ffmpeg-windows.sh, which reads this file.
# CI cross-compiles it with mingw-w64 on Ubuntu (cached until the recipe or
# this pin changes) and the Windows job tests and packages it. Nothing
# prebuilt is downloaded: the recipe is ours, so we can supply Corresponding
# Source for exactly what we ship, and no third party has to keep a binary
# online for a clean checkout to build.
#
# Minimal and LGPL: --disable-everything, then only what AACEncoder's command
# needs (raw Float32 input, resampling, the native AAC encoder, the M4A
# muxer, and the pipe its progress is read from). NO --enable-gpl, NO --enable-nonfree, NO --enable-version3, no
# external libraries; the recipe fails if its configuration says otherwise.
#
# The source pin and its SHA-256 are the ones ClipHack's recipe uses for the
# same release.
#
# LICENSE: FFmpeg's core is LGPL-2.1-or-later. The package ships its licence
# (licenses/ffmpeg-LICENSE.txt) and a note with the source below and the
# configure line (licenses/ffmpeg-SOURCE.txt).
FFMPEG_VERSION=9.0.2
FFMPEG_SOURCE_SHA256=8c3850283eb25fa026482078a04051e0be17347b09ef81a0849bec15a96e002e

# Corresponding upstream source (LGPL §6). The release date also stamps the
# exe (SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH), so the same toolchain rebuilds it byte for byte.
FFMPEG_SOURCE_RELEASE=9.0.2
FFMPEG_SOURCE_RELEASE_DATE=2026-09-17
FFMPEG_SOURCE_ARCHIVE=ffmpeg-9.0.2.tar.xz
FFMPEG_SOURCE_URL=https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-9.0.2.tar.xz
FFMPEG_SOURCE_SIGNATURE_URL=https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-9.0.2.tar.xz.asc
FFMPEG_SOURCE_GIT_TAG=n9.0.2
